---
// Page shell for /tools/compound-growth: intro + the GrowthModule island
// (compounding, savings, debt) + sources and next-tool link.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GrowthModule from "../../components/GrowthModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Growth, Savings & Debt"
  description="Compounding in every direction: how contributions, return, and time build wealth; how your savings rate (not your income) sets your timeline to financial independence; and how the same math runs in reverse as debt."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The engine: compounding</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One force runs through all of personal finance (compounding), and it works
      in every direction. Watch a balance grow from contributions, return, and time;
      see how the <strong>rate</strong> you save (not the size of your paycheck) sets
      your timeline to financial independence; then watch the same math turn against
      you as debt. Three views of one equation.
    </p>
    <div class="intro-formula" role="img" aria-label="A equals P times, open paren, 1 plus r, close paren, to the power of n">
      <span class="intro-formula-eq">A = P (1 + r)<sup>n</sup></span>
      <span class="intro-formula-key">
        <b>A</b> final amount &middot; <b>P</b> what you start with &middot;
        <b>r</b> yearly return &middot; <b>n</b> years.
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <GrowthModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Three views of one idea</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Compound growth.</strong> Money earns a return based on the amount of
          compensated risk you take, and next year that return earns a return too. Over
          decades, most of a portfolio's final value comes from growth on growth, not the
          contributions themselves.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Savings rate.</strong> How fast you reach the point where your
          investments could cover your life depends on the fraction of income you
          save, almost regardless of how much you earn, because spending and the
          nest egg you need scale together.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Debt.</strong> The exact same compounding, pointed the other way. Debt
          isn't automatically bad. Borrowing for an education or a home can pay off many
          times over in human capital and stability. But at a credit card's rate, paying
          only the minimum stretches a small balance into decades, so clearing
          high-interest debt is usually worth doing before investing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>Educational only, not financial advice.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="compound-growth" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/compound-growth" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  /* .intro-formula* styles now live in simtool.css (shared with the CAPM and
     diversification pages). */
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
